, the race stays firmly Safe Republican.", "watch": "A large national Democratic wave in 2028 or a significant third-party candidacy could narrow the margin, though changing the winner would require an unprecedented shift.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Wyoming remains the most Republican-leaning state in the country, with a presidential partisan lean of roughly 23 points toward Republicans. No public polls of the race or expert ratings are available, so the forecast rests on that structural lean. The incumbent president is term-limited, removing any incumbency effect, and no meaningful fundraising or outside-spending edge is reported for either side.\n\nHeadlines from 2026 point to a national environment that has drifted somewhat toward Democrats, but even a sizable national swing would leave a large Republican advantage in a state that has backed the Republican nominee by wide margins in every recent cycle. The forecast keeps the race firmly in the Safe Republican column, with the main uncertainty being the size of the margin rather than the winner.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.01, "margin": -23.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0232, "margin": -23.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0282, "margin": -22.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.025, "margin": -23.0}]}